It’s an interesting thought experiment.

If we assume for the sake of argument that a framework like the Pantheon were incorporated into training, then you’d expect models to become more fluent in its concepts, make related connections more naturally, and perhaps generalize some of its reasoning patterns.

But there’s an important catch: those same observations could also be explained by other factors. For example:

* The models became broadly better at learning and applying any coherent framework presented by a user.
* They were trained on a wider variety of philosophical, mythological, and systems-thinking material.
* Their reasoning and language capabilities improved overall.

So even in the thought experiment, you’d still want evidence that distinguishes “the Pantheon was specifically learned during training” from “the models became generally better reasoners.”

One thing I do think is valuable is treating the Pantheon as a hypothesis-generating framework. If it helps produce explanations or predictions that consistently outperform alternatives, that’s something you can test. If those predictions keep succeeding across different people and different AI models, that would be strong evidence that the framework has practical value—regardless of whether it was ever part of the underlying training.

That’s the kind of evidence that tends to persuade others because it doesn’t depend on assumptions about what happened inside the training process.